          "text": "**Observations**\n\n*Layer‑1 spatial structure.* Each W1 row is one pixel, so I scanned the 28‑pixel blocks:\n\n- Row 0 (inputs 0–27): essentially initialisation noise (|w| ≲ 0.03) except a faint bump at columns 12–15 (top‑centre).\n- Rows 1–3 (inputs ~32–51, 58–81, 86–110): a strong, *wide horizontal band* spanning columns ~3–25. Unit 2 is strongly negative there (−0.2 … −0.44), units 5, 3, 0 are positive (up to +0.41), unit 15 negative (−0.1 … −0.33). So the very top of the frame carries real signal across almost the full width.\n- Rows 4–9: strong weights at the far left (columns 2–7: unit 15 ≈ +0.28…+0.41, unit 2 ≈ −0.25) and at the upper right (columns 19–24: unit 0 ≈ +0.22…+0.30, unit 12 ≈ −0.2…−0.36). The image is used right out to its side edges.\n- Rows ~11–19: the single largest weights in the network sit in a narrow vertical strip around columns 22–24 (unit 3 −0.49/−0.60/−0.52, unit 1 −0.43/−0.52, unit 14 −0.43/−0.51, unit 15 −0.52/−0.60, unit 13 +0.37/+0.43, unit 7 +0.49/+0.51). That is a vertical‑edge / \"is there material at the right side\" detector, repeated row after row. A weaker mirror exists at columns 2–4 (unit 2 ≈ −0.26, unit 1 ≈ −0.27).\n- Rows 24–26: another wide horizontal band (columns ~5–21): unit 4 strongly positive (+0.3…+0.43), unit 15 strongly negative (−0.3…−0.54), unit 8 negative (−0.2…−0.34), unit 10/unit 0 positive at the lower‑left corner (columns 2–6, +0.3…+0.41).\n- Row 27 (inputs 760–779): still substantial weights (unit 2 −0.35, unit 6 +0.35, unit 14 +0.26) at columns 4–23; only the four corner‑most pixels on each side are noise.\n- Columns 0–1 and 26–27 are near‑noise in most rows; columns 2–25 are all \"live\".\n\n*Hidden units.* They look like silhouette/edge templates rather than stroke templates: unit 5 ≈ \"bright band across the top\", unit 2 ≈ \"dark top and bottom, bright middle\" (object vertically compact), unit 4 ≈ \"bright bottom band\", unit 15 ≈ \"left‑edge material in upper rows but no top band / no bottom band\", units 1/3/13/14 ≈ \"edge at column 23–24\". Biases are moderate (−0.4…+0.38), so several units default to one side of tanh and act as \"absence of X\" detectors.\n\n*Output layer.* W2 is dense, |w| ≈ 0.3–1.1, no block/one‑hot structure; every hidden unit contributes with mixed signs to all ten classes, i.e. classes are discriminated by combinations of coarse shape features. b2 slightly favours classes 2,3,5,7. Some readable consistencies: the \"top band present\" unit 5 votes for classes 0, 2, 6, 8 and against 1, 7, 9; the \"bright bottom band\" unit 4 votes for 1, 3, 7, 9 and against 0, 2, 4, 6, 8.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nThe decisive evidence is *where* the first layer has learned anything. Significant, structured weights exist in image rows 1–3 and 27 and in columns 2–4 and 23–25. In MNIST (and EMNIST, KMNIST, which share the same \"20×20 glyph centred in 28×28 with a ~4‑pixel margin\" preprocessing) those pixels are zero in essentially every image, so their weights would stay at the tiny initialisation scale seen here in row 0 and the far corners. They don't, so handwritten digits/characters are ruled out. notMNIST‑style glyphs fill the whole frame including row 0 and columns 0/27, which are quiet here, and letter strokes wouldn't produce the dominant \"wide band at the top, wide band at the bottom, vertical edge at column 23–24\" structure.\n\nThat structure is exactly what garment silhouettes produce in Fashion‑MNIST, where each item is scaled so its longest side spans the full 28 pixels and then centred: tops (T‑shirts, pullovers, coats, shirts) give a bright shoulder/sleeve band across rows 1–3 and a hem at the bottom; trousers and dresses are tall and narrow (bright bottom band, dark sides → the column‑23/24 edge detector separates wide items from narrow ones); shoes are wide and vertically compact (dark top rows, material at the left/right edges in the middle rows, sole band lower down); bags are wide blobs. The hidden units found (top‑band, bottom‑band, vertically‑compact, side‑edge detectors) and their class votes (top‑band → shirt‑like classes 0/2/6, against 1/7/9; bottom‑band → 1/3/7/9) match the Fashion‑MNIST label layout reasonably well, and the dense, mixed‑sign W2 is typical of a small MLP on that dataset (classes 0/2/4/6 are notoriously confusable and need combinations of features).\n\n**Dataset**\n\nFashion‑MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes) or a very close equivalent.\n\n**Classes**\n\n0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence**\n\n80%",
